WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:06.280
"CSI" is entirely fictional, but to give it
the feeling of reality, actress Marg Helgenberger

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.490
watched real-life autopsies.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:13.240
Keep watching to find out what stuck with
her from her encounters with cadavers.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.180
The original CSI changed television forever.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.679
It revamped the police procedural, introducing
a forensic science component that had been

00:00:20.679 --> 00:00:22.869
missing from crime dramas before then.

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:26.960
Up until that point, law enforcement shows
focused more on the down-and-dirty, street-level

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.480
aspect of fighting crime.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.610
Plots saw police officers and detectives hitting
the streets, using their gumption more than

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.489
technology to solve cases.

00:00:34.489 --> 00:00:36.910
But in 2000, science was moving forward.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.870
Years earlier, the OJ Simpson trial made the
general public more aware of how DNA evidence

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.469
worked, and people were ready to accept a
show heavy with technobabble.

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:48.340
Enter CSI: Crime Scene Investigation.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.980
The show may have gone off the air in 2015,
but it forever changed the genre.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.579
In addition to introducing a slew of spinoffs,
the beloved series launched the careers of

00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:58.820
many of its stars.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.170
Marg Helgenberger played an exotic dancer-turned-forensic
scientist named Catherine Willows.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.780
Like Dexter from Dexter, Catherine was a blood
spatter expert.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:12.490
She was able to analyze how blood fell in
a crime scene to reconstruct how a death took

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.490
place.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:19.200
But unlike most real crime scene investigators,
the agents in CSI were extremely multidisciplinary.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.810
Catherine photographed and analyzed blood
spray, but she also interrogated criminals,

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.360
put evidence through a centrifuge, and performed
autopsies.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.590
And it was while preparing for that latter
aspect of her role that Helgenberger may have

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.049
taken method acting a little too far.

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:36.790
Method acting is the process of going in-depth
with a role.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.369
For example, an actor playing an archaeologist
may study some of the basic principles so

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:45.470
that they can sound more like they know what
they're talking about during the performance.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:50.000
They may even try on a profession to better
prepare for a part, like when Tom Cruise got

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.700
a job as a UPS worker so that he could blend
in with a crowd for his role in Collateral.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.270
Marg Helgenberger actually attended autopsies
to prepare for the role of Catherine Willows

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.350
in CSI.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:04.899
The producers did not mandate this, but she
insisted that she wanted to get a more in-depth

00:02:04.899 --> 00:02:09.570
education on how the entire crime scene investigation
process worked in real life.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:12.150
In 2011, she explained to BBC Breakfast,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.410
"I asked for it and I was glad that I did
because it's something that doesn't ever leave

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.410
you.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.070
You'll always have that very strong sense
memory of the experience."

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.490
Helgenberger followed along with criminologists
in Las Vegas.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:27.650
She did a ride-along to a report of a dead
body at the Hard Rock Hotel, then was able

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:31.110
to attend the autopsy and follow the weeks-long
process.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.530
In the same interview, she said,

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.790
"I was able to track the whole case because
then I exchanged email addresses with the

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:40.500
coroner because obviously the toxicology report
takes a few weeks."

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.910
She also shared one of the more unsettling,
visceral details from her time witnessing

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.370
an autopsy being performed:

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.630
"The smell is probably the most intense thing
about it."

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.580
Unpleasant smells aside, CSI wasn't the only
show where she went method.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.200
Marg Helgenberger has had a long, lucrative
career in the industry.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.090
Even after CSI ended, she hasn't been short
on work, appearing in a litany of TV and film

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:07.310
projects, including Under the Dome, Almost
Friends, and A Dog's Journey.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:12.150
However, her most prominent role outside of
CSI would arguably have to be All Rise.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:16.610
The legal drama received critical acclaim
when it ran on OWN for two seasons before

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.030
being prematurely canceled.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.390
Helgenberger played series regular Judge Lisa
Benner, and to get in the right mindset for

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.670
the character, the actress went back to her
method acting roots.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:30.780
She explained how she prepared for the role
in an interview with AARP, and it involved

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.870
utilizing some family connections:

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:38.070
"My Georgetown Law student son interned for
a woman judge [about my age] in Pasadena.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.830
I got to sit in her courtroom and meet her.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.260
It's fascinating — the precision needed
to decide who's telling the truth and who's

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:45.530
stretching the truth."

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:49.520
She also mentions in the same interview how
All Rise allowed her to build on what she

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.140
had done during CSI.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:52.140
She elaborated,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:57.060
"I've been a lot of cops, detectives and a
criminalist on CSI, but never played a prosecutor

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.310
or a judge.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:02.620
All Rise reminds me of the early days of L.A.
Law — it has great dramatic writing but

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:03.640
also levity."

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.730
With any luck, Helgenberg's next role will
remain in the law enforcement sphere so that

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.450
she can continue putting all of this training
to good use.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:14.290
"I won't miss swabbing somebody's mouth, that's
for sure."

